BASIC(1)                       P32W Unix                        BASIC(1)

NAME
       basic - the BASIC language

SYNOPSIS
       basic
       basic program.bas

DESCRIPTION
       basic is a BASIC interpreter in the style of Microsoft BASIC.
       Given a file it loads the program, runs it and exits with
       status 0 (1 after an error). Without one it prints Ok and
       waits for lines:

       A line starting with a number is stored in the program (it
       replaces a line of the same number; the number alone deletes
       it). Any other line runs at once. Several statements may
       share a line, separated by colons. Keywords may be typed in
       any case and must stand apart from names (PRINT A, not
       PRINTA); LIST shows them in capitals.

       ^C stops a running program ("Break in 120"); CONT carries on.

NUMBERS AND STRINGS
       A number is a whole number kept exactly as a 32-bit integer
       while it fits, otherwise an IEEE double (about 15 digits, to
       1E+308): 2147483647 + 1 and 10 / 4 become doubles, 10 / 5 stays
       a whole number. PRINT shows doubles to 15 digits, .5 without a
       0, and 1E+20 form outside 15 digits.
       Constants: 42, 3.14, 1.5E-3, &HFF (hex), &O17 (octal).

       Names are letters and digits, starting with a letter, all
       significant. A$ is a string; A% always a whole number (32 bits,
       rounded when assigned); A any number. Strings may be any length.
       Arrays: A(10), B$(3, 4): DIM sets the upper bounds (from 0);
       an array used without DIM gets 10 in each dimension. A and A()
       are different.

       Operators, highest first:
              ^                 power
              - +               sign
              * /               multiply, divide
              \                 whole-number divide
              MOD               remainder
              + -               add, subtract (+ joins strings)
              = <> < > <= >=    compare (numbers or strings): -1 or 0
              NOT, AND, OR, XOR, EQV, IMP
                                on 32-bit whole numbers, bit by bit
       True is -1 and false 0; IF takes any nonzero number as true.

STATEMENTS
       [LET] v = expr            assignment
       PRINT [#n,] items         ; joins, , moves to the next 14-column
                                 zone, TAB(c) to column c, SPC(n) n
                                 spaces; a final ; or , stays on the
                                 line. ? is short for PRINT. Numbers
                                 print with a space before (or -) and
                                 after.
       PRINT [#n,] USING f$; items
                                 formatted: # digit, . point, , in the
                                 digits: thousands, $$ a floating $, **
                                 fill with *, + or - sign, ^^^^ E form;
                                 ! one character, \  \ that many, &
                                 the whole string, _ the next character
                                 as it is
       WRITE [#n,] items         values with commas, strings in quotes
       INPUT ["prompt";] v, ...  read values (, instead of ; leaves out
                                 the "? "); a wrong count or a bad
                                 number asks again ("?Redo from start")
       LINE INPUT ["prompt";] v$ a whole line
       IF e THEN stmts|line [ELSE stmts|line]
                                 (IF e GOTO line also works)
       FOR v = a TO b [STEP s] ... NEXT [v [, v]]
                                 skipped when a is already past b
       WHILE e ... WEND
       GOTO line, GOSUB line, RETURN [line]
       ON e GOTO|GOSUB line, line, ...
       DATA items, READ v, ..., RESTORE [line]
       DIM a(n [, m ...]), ...;  ERASE a, ...
       DEF FNname[(p, ...)] = expr
                                 a one-line function: FNname(args)
       SWAP v, w
       MID$(v$, i [, n]) = s$    the characters of v$ from the i-th on
                                 become the first of s$ (n of them at
                                 most, and none past the end of v$,
                                 which keeps its length)
       RANDOMIZE [n]             a new random sequence (from the
                                 clock without n)
       ON ERROR GOTO line        on an error, go to line, with ERR and
                                 ERL set; ON ERROR GOTO 0 turns it off
       RESUME [NEXT | line]      from a handler: the failed statement
                                 again, the one after it, or line
       ERROR n                   raise error n
       END, STOP                 end; stop (CONT carries on)
       REM text, ' text          a remark; ' may follow a statement
                                 on its line, with no colon
       CLS, BEEP, SLEEP n        clear the screen (VT100; in SCREEN 1
                                 the picture), bell, wait n seconds
       CLEAR                     forget the variables, close the files
       TRON, TROFF               show each line number as it runs

GRAPHICS
       On a machine with the video card, a program can draw on its
       screen: 640 by 480 dots, each lit or not. x 0, y 0 is the
       top-left dot; x runs right to 639 and y down to 479. A
       figure may be partly or wholly off the screen.

       SCREEN 1                  the screen is the picture, black;
                                 SCREEN 0: the console's text again
                                 (SCREEN 2 is taken as SCREEN 1)
       PSET (x, y) [, c]         a dot. PRESET: a dot put out
       LINE [(x1, y1)]-(x2, y2) [, [c] [, B | BF]]
                                 a line, with both its ends; without
                                 the first point, from the last one
                                 named. B: the box with those two
                                 corners; BF: the box filled
       CIRCLE (x, y), r [, [c] [, [a] [, [b] [, aspect]]]]
                                 a circle; with a and b its arc from
                                 the angle a round to b: radians, 0
                                 to the right, anticlockwise, up to
                                 2 * PI. A negative angle is that
                                 angle and its radius drawn too (two:
                                 a slice of pie). aspect: height over
                                 width, for an ellipse; r is the
                                 larger of the two
       COLOR c                   what the statements above draw in
                                 when they name no colour (1 after
                                 SCREEN 1)
       LOCATE row, column        the next line PRINT writes goes
                                 into the picture from that place:
                                 rows 1 to 30, columns 1 to 80, each
                                 character 8 dots by 16, over what
                                 was there. An INPUT's prompt goes
                                 there too; what is typed, and what
                                 is printed after, is the terminal's.
                                 In SCREEN 0 LOCATE moves the
                                 terminal's cursor (VT100)
       CLS                       the picture black
       POINT(x, y)               1 if the dot is lit, 0 if not, -1
                                 if it is off the screen

       A colour c is 0 (black: dots are put out), 1 (white) or 2,
       which turns dots over: a figure drawn twice in colour 2 is
       gone, and what was under it is as it was.

       A point may be written STEP (dx, dy): that far from the last
       point named (a circle's centre, a line's second end); in a
       line's second point, from its first.

       PRINT and INPUT stay on the terminal BASIC was started from,
       so on a serial terminal the text and the picture are both
       there. Started on the video card's own console, the text
       cannot be seen while the picture is: there the picture stays
       until a line is typed at the prompt (Return is enough), and
       then the screen is text again, as after SCREEN 0. A program
       run as  basic prog.bas  should wait before it ends (SLEEP,
       INPUT): its picture goes when it does.

       Only root and group staff may use the screen this way, one
       program at a time (video(4)). Without the card, or the right,
       SCREEN 1 is the error Device unavailable (68).

FILES AND THE SYSTEM
       OPEN f$ FOR INPUT|OUTPUT|APPEND AS [#]n
                                 n is 1 to 15
       CLOSE [[#]n, ...]         (all, without n)
       PRINT #n, WRITE #n, INPUT #n, LINE INPUT #n, EOF(n)
       KILL f$                   remove a file
       NAME a$ AS b$             rename a file
       FILES [pattern$]          list files (ls -C)
       SHELL [command$]          run a command (or a shell: exit
                                 returns to BASIC)
       SYSTEM                    leave BASIC

SQL DATABASES
       One database (sql(1)) may be open at a time, with up to four
       queries:
       SQLOPEN dir$              open the database in directory dir$
                                 (made if it is missing)
       SQLEXEC s$                run any SQL statement; SQLCHANGES is
                                 the number of rows it changed
       SQLQUERY q, s$            start query q (1 to 4): a SELECT
       SQLNEXT(q)                -1 when the next row is ready, 0 at
                                 the end
       SQLCOLS(q), SQLNAME$(q, i)
                                 the number of columns, a column's name
       SQLVAL$(q, i), SQLVAL(q, i), SQLNULL(q, i)
                                 column i (from 1) of the row as a
                                 string or a number; -1 if it is NULL
       SQLEND q, SQLCLOSE        finish a query; close the database
       A failed statement is error 70 ("SQL error: no such column:
       x"), caught by ON ERROR like any other; SQLERR$ holds the
       database's message. END, RUN, NEW and CLEAR close the database.
       /usr/ken/inventory.bas is an example.

COMMANDS
       RUN [line | "file"]       run (from line, or load file first)
       LIST [n][-[m]]            list the program or part of it
       NEW                       forget the program and the variables
       LOAD "file", SAVE "file"  .bas is added when the name has no
                                 dot; files are plain text (one line
                                 per program line, as LIST shows them)
                                 that vi or ed can edit too
       DELETE n[-m]              remove lines
       RENUM [new][,[old][,step]]
                                 renumber (10, 20, ... by default);
                                 GOTO, GOSUB, THEN, ELSE, RESTORE,
                                 RESUME and RUN follow
       CONT                      carry on after STOP or ^C

FUNCTIONS
       Numbers: ABS SGN INT (down) FIX (toward 0) CINT (nearest) CDBL
       SQR SIN COS TAN ATN EXP LOG RND PI MAX(a,b) MIN(a,b).
       RND is in [0, 1): RND(0) repeats the last, RND(-n) restarts.
       Strings: LEN(s$) ASC(s$) VAL(s$) CHR$(n) STR$(x) LEFT$(s$,n)
       RIGHT$(s$,n) MID$(s$,i[,n]) INSTR([i,]s$,t$) SPACE$(n)
       STRING$(n,c) HEX$(n) OCT$(n) UCASE$(s$) LCASE$(s$).
       Others: TIMER (seconds since midnight), TIME$ (hh:mm:ss),
       DATE$ (mm-dd-yyyy), ERR, ERL, POS(0), FRE(0), EOF(n),
       POINT(x, y) (GRAPHICS).

       INKEY$ is the key that has been pressed, one character, or
       "" if none is waiting: it does not wait, and the key is not
       shown. So
              100 K$ = INKEY$: IF K$ = "" THEN 100
       waits for a key. From the first INKEY$ until BASIC next
       wants a whole line (INPUT, its prompt) the terminal gives
       keys as they are pressed and does not echo them; ^C still
       stops the program. Return is CHR$(10); an arrow key is three
       characters, CHR$(27), "[" and a letter (A up, B down, C
       right, D left). With standard input a file or a pipe,
       INKEY$ is its next character, "" at its end.

       INPUT$(n) waits for n keys (1 to 255) and is those n
       characters, unshown like INKEY$'s: K$ = INPUT$(1) waits for
       a key, and the processor is free meanwhile, as it is not in
       the loop above. INPUT$(n, #f) is the next n characters of
       file f. With fewer left in the file, or in a standard input
       that is not a terminal, it is the error Input past end (62).

EXAMPLES
              10 INPUT "How many"; N
              20 FOR I = 1 TO N: S = S + I * I: NEXT
              30 PRINT "Sum of squares:"; S
              40 PRINT USING "Average ###.##"; S / N

       basic primes.bas > primes.txt     run a program from the shell

              10 SCREEN 1
              20 FOR R = 20 TO 200 STEP 20: CIRCLE (320, 240), R: NEXT
              30 LOCATE 16, 38: PRINT "P32W"
              40 SLEEP 5

       /usr/examples/plot.bas            a curve, its axes and labels

DIAGNOSTICS
       Errors print as Microsoft BASIC's do, with the line: Syntax
       error in 30, Type mismatch, Division by zero, Overflow, NEXT
       without FOR, RETURN without GOSUB, Out of DATA, Subscript out of
       range, Undefined line number, Illegal function call, File not
       found, Bad file number, Input past end, ... ERR holds the
       number (Microsoft's).

SEE ALSO
       sql(1), sqldb(3), softfp(3), gfx(3), video(4), vi(1), ed(1)

BUGS
       Graphics: no PAINT, DRAW, GET or PUT, no line styles, no
       VIEW or WINDOW; one screen, black and white. An arc's ends
       are whole degrees. The keywords are reserved: SCREEN, COLOR,
       POINT, CIRCLE, LOCATE, PSET and PRESET cannot be variables.
       INKEY$, INPUT$ and the SQL words are reserved too.
       PRINT USING has no ^^^^^ or $ with **, no CHAIN, COMMON, AUTO,
       EDIT or random-access files (FIELD, GET, PUT). A program line
       may be up to 1023 characters; DEF FN is one line and cannot
       recurse. Integer ^ integer is computed exactly only while the
       result fits 32 bits.
